1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,440
So how can you avoid looking like a complete idiot in an argument?

2
00:00:04,880 --> 00:00:10,040
Well, you need to learn how to build an informed argument. And the first step to that is

3
00:00:11,680 --> 00:00:16,600
understanding the question and the context to the question. So if we look back to the mountain example, if

4
00:00:17,280 --> 00:00:23,560
we had posed the question, what is the tallest mountain including below and above sea level heights,

5
00:00:23,560 --> 00:00:27,120
I would have had the chance to think, to examine my position.

6
00:00:27,880 --> 00:00:35,600
Because I would have thought that, does my statistic apply to above or just below sea level?

7
00:00:35,600 --> 00:00:41,360
And I could have understood that in the context, my statistic was irrelevant. And there were other

8
00:00:41,760 --> 00:00:48,800
possibilities to what the answer of the question could have been. And this doesn't just apply to mountains, because

9
00:00:49,120 --> 00:00:55,360
when you are building a point in a discussion, you need to know what you're saying.

10
00:00:55,520 --> 00:01:02,880
You need to understand what the question being asked is. For this question, I was let, I didn't understand

11
00:01:02,880 --> 00:01:07,200
if we were including below and above sea level, so I wasn't able to build an informed argument.

12
00:01:07,200 --> 00:01:14,000
And as we come to topics that people are more passionate about, people more often forget to look at the reasoning

13
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:19,680
and the context behind the argument, which is very important to formulating and defending an opinion.

14
00:01:20,640 --> 00:01:26,560
Secondly, you need to understand the argument from multiple and all relevant perspectives.

15
00:01:26,560 --> 00:01:33,760
So, if we go back to the Mauna Kea example again, I, Mount Everest was the only mountain that I knew the height of.

16
00:01:33,760 --> 00:01:38,240
Not the height, but I knew that it was the tallest. So it was the only mountain that I had heard of.

17
00:01:38,240 --> 00:01:45,200
But, if I had examined other mountains, I probably would have come across mountains that heights was included below and above sea level.

18
00:01:45,200 --> 00:01:50,720
And in that case, I would have been able to reevaluate my argument in the context of the question

19
00:01:50,720 --> 00:01:55,040
and understand why my sister thought that I was wrong.

20
00:01:55,040 --> 00:02:02,240
Furthermore, this applies to lots of arguments. Often people come across as ignorant or in a discussion

21
00:02:02,240 --> 00:02:07,280
because they have not considered all the people who are involved in the argument.

22
00:02:07,280 --> 00:02:13,600
Everyone, there are lots of different opinions than yours, and even an argument that can seem black and white in your opinion

23
00:02:13,600 --> 00:02:18,160
can be seen as very colorful and someone else's.

24
00:02:18,160 --> 00:02:24,320
So, in order to form an argument, you need to look at all the responsive perspectives.

25
00:02:24,320 --> 00:02:33,280
And lastly, and most importantly, in order to form an argument, you need to look at the side that's opposing yours.

26
00:02:33,280 --> 00:02:40,800
This is extremely important. In debate, we call this the con to your pro or the affirmation to your negation.

27
00:02:40,800 --> 00:02:49,120
This person inherently thinks you're wrong. The fact that there is a debate means that there's a person who thinks that you're wrong.

28
00:02:49,120 --> 00:02:54,160
And this is very important because this person has a reason why they think they're right.

29
00:02:54,160 --> 00:02:57,600
They think this reason is very right and they are going to defend it.

30
00:02:57,600 --> 00:03:04,320
Yet, if you look at the reason why you're right and discover why they are wrong, you will be able to prove your point in an argument.

31
00:03:04,320 --> 00:03:10,000
So, if we go back to the mountain example again, we can see that my sister thought she was right

32
00:03:10,000 --> 00:03:13,840
because she was looking at the statistic from below and above sea level.

33
00:03:13,840 --> 00:03:23,360
Yet, if I rephrased my argument to be that Mount Everest is the tallest mountain in the world above sea level, I would win that argument every time.

34
00:03:23,360 --> 00:03:31,920
In the status quo, when we debate, we use quick judgments and we make and we avidly defend those,

35
00:03:31,920 --> 00:03:37,440
even though we later often come to find that we were misinformed and answered it wrongly.

36
00:03:37,440 --> 00:03:45,280
Real debate requires you to slow down and become a better consumer of information in order to prove a point in an argument.

37
00:03:45,280 --> 00:03:52,080
In order to do this, you need to look at all perspectives and contexts in order to answer the question.

38
00:03:52,080 --> 00:04:00,160
And you need to look at the question in a context additionally to looking at the opposing side of the argument.

39
00:04:00,160 --> 00:04:10,320
If you do all these, you will learn to debate what's in your heart with your mind instead of letting your heart dictate how your mind perceives information.

40
00:04:10,320 --> 00:04:20,480
So, the next time you're in an argument or a discussion and you're getting frazzled, I implore you, don't raise your voice but improve your argument.

41
00:04:20,480 --> 00:04:22,960
Thank you so much. Join the debate team.

